//HTTPD    PROC M=HTTPPRM0,
//            D='SYS2.PARMLIB',
//            STCUSER=HTTPD,
//            STCGRP=USER
//*
//* HTTPD - HTTP Server STC Procedure
//*
//* Installation:
//*   Copy to SYS2.PROCLIB(HTTPD)
//*   Copy config member to SYS2.PARMLIB(HTTPPRM0)
//*   Define the STCUSER userid to RAKF (see below)
//*
//* Starting:    /S HTTPD
//*              /S HTTPD,M=HTTPPRM1     (alternate config member)
//*              /S HTTPD,D='MY.PARMLIB'  (alternate parmlib dataset)
//*              /S HTTPD,STCUSER=WEBSRV,STCGRP=WEBGRP
//*
//* STCUSER/STCGRP is the identity the server logs on to at startup.  RAKF
//* hands every started task the same STC/STCGROUP account, which holds ALTER
//* on every data set on the system, so HTTPD replaces it with one of its own
//* (issue #177).  If it is not defined the server writes HTTPD004W and keeps
//* running on the inherited identity.
//*
//* The userid needs READ on the parmlib member and on the document root.
//* It does NOT need FACILITY SVC244: HTTPD gives APF authorization back under
//* the account it took it out with, not under STCUSER.
//*
//* The symbolic is STCGRP, not STCGROUP: a JCL symbolic parameter name is at
//* most 7 characters.  The PARM keyword the program parses is STCGROUP=.
//* Commands:    /F HTTPD,DISPLAY CONFIG
//*              /F HTTPD,DISPLAY STATS
//*              /F HTTPD,HELP
//*              /F HTTPD,SHUTDOWN
//* Stopping:    /P HTTPD
//*
//* PARM is space-separated: __start() splits argv on blanks, not commas.
//HTTPD    EXEC PGM=HTTPD,REGION=8M,TIME=1440,
//            PARM='STCUSER=&STCUSER STCGROUP=&STCGRP'
//STEPLIB  DD  DISP=SHR,DSN=@LINKLIB@
//SYSUDUMP DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPDERR DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPDOUT DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPDIN  DD  DUMMY
//SNAP     DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPDBG  DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPSTAT DD  SYSOUT=*
//HTTPPRM  DD  DSN=&D(&M),DISP=SHR,FREE=CLOSE
//*
//* HASPCKPT / HASPACE1: the JES2 checkpoint and spool data sets.
//* HTTPD itself never opens them.  A CGI module that reads JES2 does,
//* and it runs in this task, so any DD it needs has to be allocated
//* here.  mvsMF 1.4.0 and later allocate both data sets themselves
//* (SYS1.HASPCKPT, SYS1.HASPACE), so the DDs are optional and are left
//* out below.  A DD statement in this procedure still wins.
//*
//* Uncomment both lines if
//*  - you run mvsMF 1.3.0 or earlier: without them every job list,
//*    job lookup and spool read answers 500 (issue #256), or
//*  - your JES2 uses a $DSNPRFX other than SYS1: the allocation only
//*    looks for the SYS1 names.
//* UNIT and VOL=SER must then match your own JES2 procedure.
//* DISP=SHR: JES2 owns these data sets.
//*HASPCKPT DD  DISP=SHR,DSN=SYS1.HASPCKPT,UNIT=3350,VOL=SER=MVS000
//*HASPACE1 DD  DISP=SHR,DSN=SYS1.HASPACE,UNIT=3350,VOL=SER=SPOOL1
